<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    labs/ctf-probe/AndroidManifest.xml

    The entrant's test APK for chapter 66 lab 04. It is complete: you should not
    need to change anything in this file. The three attributes that matter are
    the same three as in examples/vault-probe, and they are worth re-reading once
    before you start editing Java:

        package="com.pentest.ch66.ctf.test"
            a separate package, separately installed.

        android:targetPackage="com.pentest.ch66.ctf"
            the binding. This is what makes ActivityManagerService start the
            CTF app's process and load this APK's code into it.

        android:name="com.pentest.ch66.ctf.probe.CtfProbe"
            the class the framework instantiates. It must extend
            android.app.Instrumentation and needs a public no-argument
            constructor.

    build-ctf.sh signs this APK with the SAME key as the CTF target, because
    chapter 66 section 3.9 measured that the signature relationship, and not
    android:debuggable, is what ActivityManagerService checks.

    (An XML comment may not contain a double hyphen. aapt2 rejects the file with
    "not well-formed (invalid token)" and points at the line rather than the
    reason, which is why this note is here.)
-->
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    package="com.pentest.ch66.ctf.test"
    android:versionCode="1"
    android:versionName="1.0">

    <uses-sdk android:minSdkVersion="24" android:targetSdkVersion="35" />

    <instrumentation
        android:name="com.pentest.ch66.ctf.probe.CtfProbe"
        android:targetPackage="com.pentest.ch66.ctf"
        android:label="ch66 ctf probe" />

    <application android:label="ch66 ctf probe" />
</manifest>
